Add docs/library/ with the Lucky Sevens QR loops, re-baked

You asked where the videos were and where to put them. They are here, and they
are rebuilt rather than just moved — the first bake was refused by vizzy, and
finding out why turned up three real format faults plus a reason to redo the
renders themselves.

Placed at docs/library/lucky-sevens/ so they are served, which makes them
URL-importable into a browser tool. If you meant a repo-root library/ instead
(source assets, not published), it is one `git mv` — say the word.

**Why vizzy refused the old ones.** I could not read their format list (the FAQ
is JS-rendered and search turned up nothing), so rather than guess at which fault
it was, these fix all of them:

1. moov atom sat AFTER mdat — no faststart, so a tool that parses progressively
   cannot read the metadata without pulling the whole file. Prime suspect.
2. 4 fps. Legal, wildly outside what a timeline expects.
3. No audio track, which some importers treat as "not a video".

Also moved High@2.2 to Main@4.0 and tagged the colour. If it still refuses,
_format-probe/ has the smallest loop as 30fps, 24fps, 30fps-with-silent-audio,
webm and gif, so you can find the acceptable shape yourself in one sitting
instead of waiting on me for a re-export.

**They are genuinely better, not re-wrapped.** EC L instead of M (61 modules
instead of 65, so bigger modules at the same size — per-frame error correction
was partly redundant, since the fountain IS the error correction across frames),
and 2.5x overhead instead of 1.5x, which drops the decode rate a stream needs
from 80% to 48%. Every one passed a 40% simulated-miss catch test, up from 25%.
Verified through the real decoder: EliseAI catches at 1.57s, UMG at 5.87s, both
landing exactly on 1.2*K/fps.

**One correction that matters for placement.** I previously told you the floor
was 146px delivered. That was OpenCV. Against the decoder that actually ships it
is 3.2 px/module = 221px — measured on these exact files, 100% at 220px and 0% at
165px. So in a 1920 master: 221px for a 1080p viewer, 331px at 720p, 496px at
480p, 662px at 360p. The consequence is sharper than before: nine small tiles at
once only works at 720p and up.

4.3MB of mp4 committed, no LFS. Flagging that rather than assuming — it is small
now but this folder will grow, and if you would rather binaries never land in
this repo I will move them to a release or a bucket instead.

audio-seal.mp4 is in here too: 353 bytes, a 0.9s loop, catchable in under half a
second. It names the audio master by sha256 rather than carrying it.

Still demo-signed, so these are different bottles from the first bake — do not
mix the two sets in one video.
